**Somewhat, but only clearly at three months ahead.** One month ahead, simple models cut the error by about 12–13%, but that gain isn't statistically significant at the 5% level. Three months ahead, they cut it by about 28%, and that gain is significant.

**How I tested it:** I took monthly log changes in CPIAUCSL from 1990 to October 2026 (440 months). Each model was re-fitted on all data up to each forecast date, giving 264 forecast dates from October 2004 onward. Each model was compared with the no-change forecast using root-mean-square error (RMSE) and Diebold–Mariano tests, which check whether the gap is significant.

**Is there anything to predict?** Yes. Inflation this month is correlated with last month's (correlation 0.46), and that correlation is clearly not chance (Ljung–Box p<1e-4). Volatility also clusters (ARCH test p<1e-4), and the series has fat tails (kurtosis 12).

